= Module 1: Surface Transport — Road Networks & Strategic Corridors

== 1.1 Historical Evolution and Road Classification in India
India possesses the second-largest road network in the world, aggregating approximately 6.37 million kilometres. Roads play a vital catalytic role in establishing door-to-door physical connectivity, integrating remote rural hinterlands with urban industrial growth poles, and providing feeder connectivity to railways, inland ports, and airports.

Prior to Independence, modern road development commenced with the landmark *Nagpur Plan (1943)*, which proposed a twenty-year roadmap for classifying roads into four distinct functional tiers:
- *1. National Highways (NH):* Primary trunk routes traversing the length and breadth of the country, interconnecting major ports, state capitals, strategic border frontiers, and industrial hubs. Constructed and maintained by the Central Government via the Ministry of Road Transport and Highways (MoRTH) and the *National Highways Authority of India (NHAI)*, operationalized under the NHAI Act, 1988.
- *2. State Highways (SH):* Secondary arterial links connecting district headquarters with the state capital, national highways, and important commercial centres. Constructed and financed by State Public Works Departments (PWDs).
- *3. Major District Roads (MDR):* Roads providing vital nodal connectivity between production centres, agricultural markets (mandis), and district headquarters, maintained by Zilla Parishads and state PWDs.
- *4. Rural Roads (Other District and Village Roads):* Essential grassroots conduits linking villages with higher-order road networks. Substantially upgraded under the *Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY)* launched in December 2000 to provide all-weather paved connectivity to eligible unconnected rural habitations.

