== 1.4 Spatial Distribution of Road Density in India
Road density is defined as the total length of roads per 100 square kilometres of geographic surface area. In India, road density exhibits stark spatial variations driven by relief, topography, economic development, and historical urbanization:
- *High Density States:* Kerala (exceeding 500 km per 100 sq km) and Goa, followed by the fertile alluvial plains of Uttar Pradesh, Punjab, Haryana, and West Bengal. These regions feature flat terrain, dense rural populations, intensive agriculture, and extensive industrialization.
- *Low Density Regions:* The high-altitude Himalayan belt (Ladakh, Jammu & Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, and Arunachal Pradesh) and hyper-arid western Rajasthan exhibit low road density (often below 20 to 30 km per 100 sq km) due to steep gradients, rugged terrain, forest tracts, frequent landslides, and sparse populations.

= Module 2: Indian Railways — Track Gauge Evolution & Freight Corridors

== 2.1 Historical Legacy and Network Dimensions
The first commercial passenger railway train in India steamed over a distance of *34 kilometres between Bombay (Bori Bunder) and Thane on April 16, 1853*, operated by the Great Indian Peninsula Railway. Since then, Indian Railways has expanded into the lifelines of the national economy, serving as the fourth-largest national railway network globally under single management.

Administratively, the immense network of Indian Railways is organized into *18 operational railway zones* (including the Kolkata Metro and South Coast Railway), each headed by a General Manager reporting to the Railway Board.

