== 2.2 Track Gauge Classification and Project Unigauge
Track gauge refers to the clear minimum perpendicular distance between the inner faces of the two running rails on a railway track. Historically, three distinct gauge widths were deployed across India:

#definition-box(title: "The Three Railway Gauges in India")[
  1. *Broad Gauge (1.676 metres / 5 ft 6 in):* The heavy-capacity standard gauge of India. Broad gauge allows high operating speeds, heavy axle loading, and high freight volumes. It accounts for *over 90%* of total route kilometrage.
  2. *Metre Gauge (1.000 metre / 3 ft 3 3/8 in):* Extensively laid in non-mountainous drylands and peripheral areas during the colonial era to minimize initial capital expenditure.
  3. *Narrow Gauge (0.762 metre / 2 ft 6 in and 0.610 metre / 2 ft):* Confined exclusively to rugged mountain railways (e.g., Kalka-Shimla, Darjeeling Himalayan, Nilgiri Mountain Railway, and Matheran Hill Railway).
]

#note-box(title: "Strategic Impact of Project Unigauge")[
  Launched in the 1990s, *Project Unigauge* aims to systematically convert all existing Metre Gauge and Narrow Gauge lines into uniform Broad Gauge. Eliminating multi-gauge transshipment points has reduced cargo transit delays, removed pilferage and break-of-gauge damage, cut operational overheads, and allowed seamless pan-Indian container train operations (CONCOR).
]

== 2.3 The Konkan Railway: An Engineering Marvel
Commissioned in *1998*, the *Konkan Railway* is one of the most remarkable engineering accomplishments in Indian transport history. Spanning a distance of *760 kilometres*, it traverses the ecologically sensitive and rugged terrain of the Western Ghats, linking *Roha in Maharashtra* to *Thokur (near Mangaluru) in Karnataka*, cutting across Goa.

#cheatsheet-box(title: "Core Architectural Facts of the Konkan Railway Corridor")[
  - *Engineering Scale:* Traverses 146 major rivers and streams, passes through 2,000 bridges, and cuts through *91 mountain tunnels*.
  - *Longest Tunnel:* The *Karbude Tunnel* near Ratnagiri measures approximately *6.5 kilometres* in length.
  - *Tallest Viaduct:* The *Panval Nadi Viaduct* near Ratnagiri rises to an extraordinary height of *64 metres*, rivaling multi-story towers.
  - *Economic Impact:* Reduced rail travel time between Mumbai and Mangaluru by *over 12 hours*, unlocking mineral, agro-horticultural, and tourist growth across coastal Konkan.
  - *Technical Innovation:* Successfully implemented the pioneering *Roll-on/Roll-off (RO-RO)* service, enabling fully loaded cargo trucks to be carried directly on railway flat wagons, economizing fuel and minimizing highway accidents.
]

== 2.4 Dedicated Freight Corridors (DFCs)
To decongest saturated trunk rail corridors and accelerate average freight train speeds from 25 km/h to over 65 km/h, the Ministry of Railways established the Dedicated Freight Corridor Corporation of India Limited (DFCCIL) to construct high-axle-load electrified freight-only lines:
- *Western DFC (1,504 km):* Connects Dadri in Uttar Pradesh to Jawaharlal Nehru Port (JNPT) near Mumbai, passing through Haryana, Rajasthan, Gujarat, and Maharashtra. Facilitates rapid container transit from northern industrial hubs to western ports.
- *Eastern DFC (1,856 km):* Connects Sahnewal (Ludhiana) in Punjab to Dankuni near Kolkata in West Bengal. Primarily transports thermal coal from eastern mineral belts to northern power stations, alongside finished steel and agricultural commodities.

= Module 3: Waterways, Pipeline Infrastructure & Civil Aviation

== 3.1 Inland Water Transport and National Waterways (IWAI)
Inland water transport represents the most fuel-efficient, environmentally benign, and cost-effective mode of moving bulk cargo over long distances. Unlike roads and railways, waterways require no capital expenditure for constructing track beds, requiring only river training works, dredging, and navigation locks.

The *Inland Waterways Authority of India (IWAI)* was statutorily established in *October 1986* with headquarters at Noida (UP) to manage, develop, and regulate statutory National Waterways.

