== 6.2 Space Technology: INSAT and IRS Satellite Systems
India's indigenous space capabilities, orchestrated by the *Indian Space Research Organisation (ISRO)*, have transformed communication networks, weather forecasting, disaster mitigation, and natural resource governance. ISRO operates two distinct operational satellite constellations:

#definition-box(title: "The Twin Pillars of India's Space Program: INSAT and IRS")[
  - *1. INSAT (Indian National Satellite System):* A constellation of multi-purpose geostationary satellites stationed approximately 36,000 kilometres above the Earth. Commissioned in *1983* with the launch of INSAT-1B, INSAT is dedicated to *telecommunications, television broadcasting, meteorological earth observation, disaster warning, and search-and-rescue operations*. It powers rural telephony networks and distance education (EDUSAT).
  - *2. IRS (Indian Remote Sensing Satellite System):* A fleet of advanced earth-observation satellites operating in Sun-Synchronous Polar Orbits at altitudes of 600 to 900 kilometres. Commissioned with the launch of IRS-1A in *1988*. Data acquired by IRS sensors is downlinked and processed by the *National Remote Sensing Centre (NRSC) in Hyderabad*. IRS provides multispectral imagery essential for *groundwater exploration, soil salinity mapping, crop acreage estimation, forest canopy monitoring, flood damage assessment, and mineral prospecting*.
]

